//  A SURFACE MOD THAT EXISTS FOR A FRACTION OF A SECOND.
//
//  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL. There is no way for a script to take a surface
//  mod OFF a tile in retail. Both direct routes are closed in the engine, and
//  both were closed by reading the source rather than by guessing:
//
//    world.placeMod  ->  WorldImpl::canPlaceMod opens with
//                        `if (!isRealMod(mod)) return false;` and NoModId is a
//                        meta mod, so "metamod:none" can never be placed. The
//                        allowOverlap flag is not even consulted on the
//                        PlaceMod branch of validateTileModification, which is
//                        why passing true and false gave identical refusals.
//
//    world.damageTiles -> WorldImpl::tileDamageParameters, for a mod with
//                        breaksWithTile TRUE, returns
//                        modDamageParameters(mod).sum(materialDamageParameters(target)).
//                        The mod's health is an ADDEND to the block's, so
//                        killing asterite kills the obsidian under it. That is
//                        the ore-vein rule.
//
//  SO REMOVAL IS TWO STEPS, AND THIS IS THE THING IN THE MIDDLE. A real mod
//  can be written over another one -- proven in game 2026-09-11, "gold" and
//  "silver" both overwrote asterite from a monster script. And a mod with
//  breaksWithTile FALSE takes the fourth branch of that same function:
//
//                        return modDamageParameters(mod);
//
//  its own damage pool, alone, with the tile untouched. So the unit places
//  this over the deposit and then breaks it, and what is left is a bare tile
//  carrying no mod at all -- which is what could not be done in one call.
//
//  MODELLED ON flowerygrass, which is what put us onto it: swapping asterite
//  for it made the deposit breakable, and the only difference between them is
//  this flag. Vanilla grass works the same way and always has -- the first
//  swing strips the grass, the next breaks the dirt.
//
//  NOTHING OF OURS IS LEFT IN THE WORLD, and that is the point of the second
//  step. An earlier version kept breaksWithTile true and simply left this mod
//  on the tile forever: invisible, harmless, and permanent litter that also
//  had to be tuned so it did not change how the host block mined by hand.
//
//  EVERY FIELD BELOW IS EITHER LOAD-BEARING OR DELIBERATELY ABSENT.
//
//  modId 58700, claimed on Modding:Matmods:Mods. Checked 2026-09-11: the
//  highest claim on that table was 57051-57055 and nothing sat at 58700. Well
//  clear of the meta mod ids at the top of the range, which is what isRealMod
//  tests against.
//
//  breaksWithTile FALSE -- BY OMISSION, EXACTLY AS flowerygrass DOES IT. The
//  default is false and writing it out would suggest the value had been
//  weighed against something. It is the whole mechanism; if this file ever
//  grows a `"breaksWithTile" : true` the removal silently becomes permanent
//  litter again and the only symptom is cleared tiles keeping an invisible
//  mod.
//
//  health 0, SO ANY DAMAGE AT ALL DESTROYS IT. flowerygrass ships the same
//  value for the same reason. The unit still sizes its damage from this number
//  rather than assuming it -- see the clearing damage in petportsTaskAction --
//  so raising it here raises the blow that removes it.
//
//  NO renderTemplate, so no modRenderProfile is built and nothing is drawn.
//  MaterialDatabase guards that read with `if (modConfig.contains(...))`, so
//  its absence is a supported state rather than a gamble. It matters less than
//  it did now that this exists for one frame, but a failed damage step leaves
//  it visible and it should still be nothing.
//
//  NO itemDrop, so breaking it yields nothing. The ore the unit mined is
//  synthesised into its cargo; this mod must never be a second source of it,
//  and it is now something that actually gets broken.
//
//  NO miningSounds or miningParticle. MaterialDatabase::miningSound falls back
//  to the HOST MATERIAL when the mod declares none, so breaking this plays
//  whatever the block underneath sounds like -- which is the right noise for
//  the last moment of a mine, and one we do not have to choose.

{
	"modId" : 58700,
	"modName" : "petports_cleared",
	"description" : "Nothing at all, and not for long.",

	"health" : 0,
	"harvestLevel" : 0
}
